To create a Discord bot with the `ban` command using Discord.js v14, follow these steps:

1. Set up a Discord developer account and create a new bot application.

2. Add the bot to your desired server and note down the bot token.

3. Install the latest version of Discord.js (`npm install discord.js`) and initialize a new Node.js project.

4. In your main Discord bot file (e.g., `index.js`), import the required modules:

```javascript
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits } = require('discord.js');
const { token } = require('./config.json'); // Replace './config.json' with your preferred file name and path
```

5. Create a new Discord client instance:

```javascript
const client = new Client({
  intents: [
    GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
    GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
    GatewayIntentBits.DirectMessages,
    GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
  ],
});
```

6. Register the `ban` command using the `ApplicationCommandManager` provided by Discord.js:

```javascript
client.on('ready', async () => {
  const command = {
    name: 'ban',
    description: 'Bans a user from the server.',
    type: 1, // ApplicationCommandType.ChatInput
    options: [
      {
        name: 'user',
        description: 'The user to ban.',
        type: 6, // ApplicationCommandOptionType.User
        required: true,
      },
      {
        name: 'days',
        description: 'Number of days to ban the user.',
        type: 4, // ApplicationCommandOptionType.Integer
        required: false,
      },
    ],
    defaultPermission: false, // Required for bot owners to use the command
  };

  const commandData = await client.application.commands.create({
    command,
    guildId: 'YOUR_SERVER_ID_HERE', // Replace with your server ID
  });

  console.log(`Command registered: ${commandData.name}`);
});
```

7. Handle the command's interaction event:

```javascript
client.on('interactionCreate', async interaction => {
  if (!interaction.isChatInputCommand()) return;

  if (interaction.commandName === 'ban') {
    try {
      const user = interaction.options.getUser('user');
      const days = interaction.options.getInteger('days', true);

      await interaction.guild.members.ban(user, { days });

      await interaction.reply({
        content: `Successfully banned ${user.tag} for ${days || 'permanent'}.`,
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      await interaction.reply({
        content: 'An error occurred while banning the user.',
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    }
  }
});
```

8. Start the Discord client:

```javascript
client.login(token);
```

9. Create a `.env` file at the root of your project and add your Discord bot token:

```
BOT_TOKEN=<your-discord-bot-token>
```

10. Create a `config.json` file at the root of your project and add your bot token:

```json
{
  "token": process.env.BOT_TOKEN
}
```

11. Run your Discord bot using `node index.js`.

Note: Make sure to replace `YOUR_SERVER_ID_HERE` with your server's actual ID.

With these steps, your Discord bot will be able to handle the `/ban` command as a slash command. Bot owners will need to manually grant the bot the `Ban Members` permission in the server settings for the command to work.

Here's an example implementation of a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing Markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <regex>
#include "MarkdownToHtml.h" // Assuming you have a Markdown-to-HTML conversion library available, e.g. https://github.com/tomLane/cpp-markdown

// Function to convert Markdown to HTML
std::string convertMarkdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    // Convert Markdown to HTML using a Markdown-to-HTML library
    cpp_markdown::Converter converter;
    converter.Convert(markdown, html);

    // Apply some basic HTML formatting, depending on your needs
    std::regex italic_regex("\\*([^*]*)\\*");
    std::regex bold_regex("__([^_]+)__");
    std::regex link_regex("\\[([^\\]]+)]\\(([^\\)]+)\\)");
    std::regex header_regex("^#{1,6} ([^#]+)$");

    std::string replaced_markdown = markdown;
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, italic_regex, "<em>$1</em>");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, bold_regex, "<strong>$1</strong>");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, link_regex, "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");
    std::smatch match;
    while (std::regex_search(replaced_markdown, match, header_regex)) {
        std::string level = std::to_string(match[0].length());
        std::string header_text = match[1];
        replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, match[0], "<h" + level + ">" + header_text + "</h" + level + ">");
    }

    // Replace any remaining Markdown characters with their corresponding HTML entities
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\`"), "&#96;");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\*"), "&#42;");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\_"), "&#95;");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\~"), "~");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\|"), "&#124;");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\#"), "&#35;");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\\\"), "\\\\");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\`{3}"), "<pre>");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\`{3}\\s*\\</pre\\s*>"), "</pre>");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\`{1}"), "`");
    replaced_markdown = std::regex_replace(replaced_markdown, std::regex("\\\\[\\]()"), "[ ](");

    html = replaced_markdown;
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "Hello **world**! [Google](https://google.com/#q=cpp_markdown) is a #1 search engine.";
    std::string html = convertMarkdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl; // Output: "<p><em>Hello</em> <strong>world</strong>! <a href=\"https://google.com/#q=cpp_markdown\">Google</a> is a <h1>search engine</h1></p>"
    return 0;
}
```

This implementation uses the `cpp_markdown` library to convert the Markdown input to HTML. It then applies some basic formatting, including:

- Italicization: `\*text\*` becomes `<em>text</em>`
- Bold: `__text__` becomes `<strong>text</strong>`
- Links: `[text](url)` becomes `<a href="url">text</a>`
- Headers: `# text` becomes `<h1>text</h1>` (up to 6 hash symbols for headers 1-6)

Finally, it replaces any remaining Markdown characters with their corresponding HTML entities, such as backticks (`\`) and vertical bars (`\|`) to prevent them from being interpreted as Markdown syntax.

Note that this implementation assumes you have a Markdown-to-HTML conversion library available (e.g. `cpp_markdown`, as shown here). If you prefer to implement your own Markdown parser, you'll need to modify this implementation accordingly.

